 "scenes": [
  {
   "id": "intro",
   "steps": [
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "A priority queue keeps handing out its most important item. The binary heap does that in logarithmic time, inside a plain array, with no pointers at all, and it also gives a guaranteed n log n sort. This video builds one."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "write",
       "target": "head"
      },
      {
       "action": "replace",
       "target": "head",
       "text": "Binary heaps"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Here are the seven stages."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "show",
       "target": "stages"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "The heap property, sifting down, building a heap, and why building takes only linear time."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "stages[0..=3]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Then insert and extract-max, heapsort, and an exam question."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "stages[4..=6]"
      }
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "prop",
   "steps": [
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Stage 1: a binary heap is a complete binary tree, stored in an array from index 1, with the parent and child formulas from video 1."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "replace",
       "target": "head",
       "text": "Stage 1 of 7: the heap property"
      },
      {
       "action": "hide",
       "target": "stages"
      },
      {
       "action": "show",
       "target": "hp"
      },
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "hp[0..=1]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "In a max-heap, every parent is at least as large as its children."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "hp[2]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "So the largest key is always at the root."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "hp[3]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "But a heap is not sorted: nothing orders two siblings, or two different subtrees. It is a much weaker, and much cheaper, arrangement than a search tree."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "hp[4]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "A min-heap is the mirror image: every parent at most its children, and the minimum at the root. Everything in this video works for it with the comparisons reversed."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "hp[2]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Its height is the floor of log 2 of n, because it is complete."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "hp[5]"
      },
      {
       "action": "write",
       "target": "takeaway"
      },
      {
       "action": "replace",
       "target": "takeaway",
       "text": "parent ≥ children · max at A[1]"
      }
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "heapify",
   "steps": [
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Stage 2: MAX-HEAPIFY fixes one node whose subtrees are already heaps. Here, the node at index 2 holds 4, smaller than its children 14 and 7."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "replace",
       "target": "head",
       "text": "Stage 2 of 7: sifting down"
      },
      {
       "action": "hide",
       "target": "takeaway"
